  data: {
    LLM: "Always look for plays that sum to 10 or 15. The 5-5 (double-five) tile is the strongest opener as it scores 10 points immediately.\n\nBlocking: If you have many 4s but your opponent is low, try to keep an open 4 on the board while matching other ends to suits you control.\n\nThe 'End Control': If three ends are 6, 4, and 5 (Total 15), look for a play that changes the 6/4 to something that keeps the total as a multiple of 5 (e.g., matching the 6 with a 6-1 to make the ends 1, 4, 5 = 10).",
    Player: "Always look for plays that sum to 10 or 15. The 5-5 (double-five) tile is the strongest opener as it scores 10 points immediately.\n\nBlocking: If you have many 4s but your opponent is low, try to keep an open 4 on the board while matching other ends to suits you control.\n\nThe 'End Control': If three ends are 6, 4, and 5 (Total 15), look for a play that changes the 6/4 to something that keeps the total as a multiple of 5 (e.g., matching the 6 with a 6-1 to make the ends 1, 4, 5 = 10).",
